  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nSHOWER/THUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nLATE TONIGHT INTO THURSDAY MORNING...Can't rule out a few spotty \nhigh-based showers/sprinkles and perhaps a clap of thunder late \ntonight into Thursday morning, mainly along/northwest of a line \nextending from Hutchinson to Salina, within a zone of modest mid-\nlevel moisture and convergence. Very limited instability and meager \nforcing should preclude anything more than very light and/or trace \namounts of moisture, and most locations will likely remain dry. \nSince activity will be very light/spotty, kept the forecast dry.\n\nLATE THURSDAY AFTERNOON-EVENING...Subtle mid-upper energy \napproaching a weak dryline circulation amidst slightly richer low-\nlevel moisture may be enough to breach a weak cap for isolated \nthunderstorm initiation late Thursday, mainly west of a line \nextending from Lincoln to Lyons to Kingman to Anthony. There is some \nCAM support for this scenario as well. Given the uncertainty and \nvery spotty nature, kept the forecast dry for now.\n\nLATE TUESDAY-THURSDAY...Per model consensus, deep western CONUS \nupper troughing finally ejects northeast across the northern CONUS \nearly next week, which will attempt to drive a weak cold front south \ninto Mid-America. This could support an increase in thunderstorm \nchances across the Kansas region, especially if the more bullish \nECMWF solution verifies. However, there remains quite a bit of \nuncertainty with regard to the cold front's southern extent. The GFS \nand Canadian models keep this feature mainly north of the region and \nsupports a continued mostly dry forecast. Stay tuned as we refine \nforecast details in the coming days.  \n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nWith anomalous upper level high pressure remaining anchored across \nthe central and southern CONUS, above average temperatures look to \ncontinue through at least Tuesday or Wednesday of next week, with \nforecast high temperatures ranging from the 98-103 degree range \nacross central and southeast KS. A change from the last several days \nwill be slightly backed low-level flow supporting slightly richer \nlow-level moisture streaming north into the region. This will inch \nafternoon through early evening heat indices closer to the 105 \ndegree threshold for many locations, so will go ahead and expand the \nheat advisory to include all of central and southeast KS through \nFriday. This will also better match up with surrounding NWS forecast \noffices. Later shifts may need to consider extending through the \nweekend or early next week.",
